  "factCheck": {
    "status": "verified",
    "by": "HeyMariner daily desk",
    "checkedAt": "2026-11-16",
    "notes": "Rule 26(b): trawling = two all-round lights in a vertical line, green over white; masthead light abaft of and higher than the all-round green (vessel under 50 m not obliged); making way: sidelights and sternlight. Rule 26(c): fishing other than trawling = all-round red over white; outlying gear extending more than 150 m horizontally: an all-round white light or a cone apex upwards in the direction of the gear; making way: sidelights and sternlight. Day shape for both: two cones apexes together (not shown). Rule 18(a)(iii): a power-driven vessel underway keeps out of the way of a vessel engaged in fishing (subject to Rules 9, 10 and 13). Pilot vessel Rule 29: white over red all-round. Mnemonics are memory aids, not rule text. Diagram trawler drawn with the masthead light added separately (render preset shows two masthead lights)."
  },
  "meta": {
    "description": "How to tell a trawler from another fishing vessel at night under COLREG Rule 26: green over white means trawling, red over white means fishing by other means, and a third white light shows outlying gear beyond 150 metres. A power-driven vessel keeps out of the way under Rule 18, and red over white must not be confused with the pilot vessel's white over red.",
